import crate::parse::parser::Parser
import redox_lexer::TokenKind

-- | Whether the three tokens at the cursor begin an `over` — plan 88 leaf 3.
-- |
-- | ```text
-- | over (.addr.city) .to_uppercase u   -- an update
-- | over (x)                            -- a call to a function named `over`
-- | over = 3                            -- a binding named `over`
-- | ```
-- |
-- | **The word, `(`, then `.`** — and the third token is what makes leaving
-- | `over` unreserved safe. A parenthesised selector is the only thing in the
-- | language that opens `(.`, because a leading `.` inside parentheses is a
-- | field selector or a method reference and never an operand. One token would
-- | claim every binding named `over`; two would claim every call applied to a
-- | parenthesised argument.
-- |
-- | `over` is the **fourth** contextual word, after `from`, `pattern` and
-- | `template`, and plan 90's three fixity words make it the seventh of the
-- | kind. The finding those share: a word recognised in exactly one position
-- | does not need to be unusable in every other, and plan 87 §10.4 paid for
-- | learning that the other way round.
-- |
-- | Sufficient rather than diagnostic. When this answers `true`,
-- | `over_update` re-reads the selector and is the one that reports `RDX0127`
-- | — so `over (.a) …` with something wrong after it gets a diagnostic about
-- | the update, which is what the author meant to write.
export(crate) opens_an_over : &Parser -> bool
opens_an_over p =
  word_is_over = case &(.peek p).kind of
    TokenKind::Ident text -> text == "over"
    _ -> false
  if !word_is_over
    return false
  opens = .is_some_and (\t -> t.kind == TokenKind::LParen) (.peek_at 1 p)
  selects = .is_some_and (\t -> t.kind == TokenKind::Dot) (.peek_at 2 p)
  opens && selects
